# Triad-Lurch Garage

**Ingredients:** GEORGIAN PANDURI + UK GARAGE / 2-STEP + SUB-BASS + FRAME DRUM / HANDCLAPS + ELECTRIC PIANO + BASS TROMBONE

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a spring residency in a converted municipal dispatch office on the edge of Tbilisi, where panduri player Nino Beridze rehearsed clipped three-string chord figures while London producer Jay Mercer rebuilt a set of sparse 2-step sketches from shuffled hats, displaced snares, sub-bass, and short vocal cuts. Their first attempt simply layered folk strumming over garage drums and collapsed into rhythmic clutter. The breakthrough came when Nino separated her right-hand pattern into three distinct attack roles: thumbed low-string pulse, middle-string chord shell, and bright upper-string answer. Jay mapped the garage groove around those three attack classes instead of around a fixed strum. The panduri therefore stopped behaving like accompaniment and began functioning as a living three-lane sequencer. Frame drum, electric piano, and bass trombone were added only after the hand roles were established. The result became a dance form where UK garage swing is reinforced by physical three-string choreography rather than imposed on top of it.

Triad-Lurch Garage is a dry, elastic acoustic-electronic style built from Georgian panduri articulation and the asymmetrical pulse of UK garage and 2-step. It normally runs between 128 and 136 BPM in 4/4. The electronic rhythm is sparse: compact kick, clipped snare, swung hats, occasional rim or clap, short sub-bass notes, and brief vocal fragments. The panduri provides the upper rhythmic identity through three-string chord shells, muted upstrokes, low-string thumb pulses, hammer-on ornaments, and brief pentatonic figures.

The core aesthetic is **three hand roles against one swung grid**. The panduri does not play continuous folk strumming. Each string course is assigned a functional category, and those roles interact with the electronic groove independently. The low course can imply kick or bass weight, the middle course can define chord identity, and the upper course can create offbeat sparkle or syncopated replies. The three functions may align, separate, or trade positions across a two-bar garage pattern.

The panduri is recorded with one small-diaphragm condenser angled toward the soundboard between bridge and neck joint, one ribbon microphone 50–70 cm back for body warmth, and an optional contact pickup used only for muted strokes and low-level percussive detail. A restrained stereo room pair may appear in breakdowns. Compression remains light enough that differences between thumb, nail, muted rake, and open chord attack stay obvious.

Players favor open fifths, triadic fragments, suspended seconds, major and minor thirds, sixths, modal pentatonic cells, drone-string reinforcement, and short hammer-on or pull-off ornaments. Dense chord changes are avoided. Most pieces remain in one or two modal centers for long stretches so the interest comes from hand-role movement and rhythmic displacement.

The primary compositional device is the **Three-Lane Strum**. Every panduri figure is split into LOW, MID, and HIGH attack classes. LOW attacks may coincide with kick or sub-bass, MID attacks may define harmony near the snare, and HIGH attacks may occupy swung offbeats or empty spaces. A written phrase is therefore not just a chord rhythm; it is a three-part percussion-harmony pattern distributed across one instrument.

A second device, **Lurch Swap**, changes the rhythmic job of one string course without changing the chord shape. For example, the LOW string may stop landing with the kick and move onto the swung offbeat while the HIGH string takes over the downbeat accent. The harmony sounds familiar, but the physical center of the groove shifts sideways.

A third device, **Ghost Step**, reserves one expected garage drum event for a muted panduri attack instead. A missing snare may be replaced by a dry nail click and damped chord scrape; a missing kick may be implied by a strong thumbed low-string pluck. The acoustic event must be strong enough that the listener feels the absent machine hit without hearing it.

A fourth device, **Triad Slip**, delays only the harmonic part of the panduri gesture. The low string may strike on time, but the middle and high strings arrive a swung sixteenth later. The resulting chord seems to unfold sideways across the beat. The effect is not a flam generated in software; it comes from deliberate right-hand sequencing.

A fifth device, **Window Bar**, clears sub-bass, electric piano, and most drums for one bar while the panduri continues the same Three-Lane Strum. Because the acoustic pattern contains low, middle, and high functions internally, the groove should remain recognizable even when the electronic support disappears.

UK garage drums remain sparse and sharp. Kick is short and punchy, often avoiding beat one or appearing as displaced doubles. Snare or clap is dry and normally placed on the familiar 2-step backbone while occasionally being omitted for Ghost Step substitution. Hats use swung eighths and sixteenths with velocity variation, short open-hat lifts, and abrupt gaps. Percussion density stays lower than house or breakbeat; empty space is essential.

Sub-bass is short, rounded, and mostly mono. Lines use tonic, fifth, flat seventh, octave, and occasional second-degree or chromatic pickup notes. Bass phrases often answer LOW panduri attacks rather than doubling them continuously. Long 808 glides, drill slides, and sustained cinematic sub drops are excluded.

Frame drum supplies organic low-mid punctuation through muted center strokes, rim taps, fingertip rolls, and dry slaps. It does not play a continuous traditional groove. Instead, it appears at section boundaries, Ghost Steps, and Window Bars, connecting the hand-played attack of the panduri with the electronic rhythm chassis.

Handclaps are close, dry, and small. They may reinforce selected snare hits or arrive slightly late in choruses. Multiple clap layers should retain human spread rather than becoming a giant pop stack.

Electric piano provides sustained harmonic support without competing with the panduri’s attack pattern. Rhodes- or Wurlitzer-like voicings favor minor 7, major 7, sus2, add9, open fifths, and compact dominant 9 shells. Chords appear mainly in longer note values. Tremolo and chorus remain subtle.

Bass trombone supplies low acoustic punctuation rather than a horn section. It uses short tonic or fifth stabs, breath-heavy swells, half-step approaches, falls, and occasional two-note responses. The instrument is particularly effective after Window Bars, where one low brass entry can make the return of the full groove feel large without adding harmonic density.

Vocals are optional but useful. Georgian and English fragments may coexist. Delivery should be intimate, clipped, or rhythmically conversational: short lines, call-and-response syllables, whispered pickups, or garage-style vocal cuts. Lyrics work best around dispatch calls, hillside streets, taxi meters, apartment windows, wet pavement, stairwells, late arrivals, and repeated routes.

Effects remain compact. Panduri receives short room ambience and optional small plate. Electric piano can use mild chorus and short stereo delay. Bass trombone gets a dark room. Vocals can use small plate and tightly filtered delay throws. Drum ambience remains short. Avoid lush folk panoramas, orchestral Caucasian textures, festival EDM builds, trance pads, trap hat rolls, giant reverbs, and synthetic panduri layers.

The defining tension is physical hand choreography against programmed swing. The machine supplies a lurching floor; the panduri reveals that three different rhythmic jobs can live inside a single chord gesture.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 128–136 BPM, primarily 4/4 UK garage / 2-step with sparse displaced kicks, clipped snare or clap, swung hats, short sub-bass, and a panduri pattern divided into low-, mid-, and high-string rhythmic functions.

**Harmonic Palette:** Modal major/minor centers, open fifths, suspended seconds, minor and major thirds, sixths, minor 7, major 7, add9, dominant 9 shells, drone-string reinforcement, and short pentatonic ornaments.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Three-Lane Strum** — LOW, MID, and HIGH panduri attacks each receive a distinct rhythmic or harmonic function inside the garage groove.
2. **Lurch Swap** — one string course changes rhythmic role without changing the chord shape, shifting the perceived center of the pattern.
3. **Ghost Step** — an expected machine hit is deliberately omitted and replaced by a muted or accented panduri event.
4. **Triad Slip** — the low string attacks first while middle and high chord tones arrive a swung subdivision later through real right-hand sequencing.
5. **Window Bar** — electronic bass, keys, and most drums disappear for one bar while the panduri alone preserves the recognizable groove architecture.
6. **No-Strum-Wallpaper Rule** — the panduri cannot be reduced to continuous folk strumming or decorative arpeggiation; each string course must have an audible functional role.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through denser Lurch Swaps, stronger thumbed low-string attacks, brighter upper-string replies, more frequent clap reinforcement, short bass-trombone punctuation, and faster changes between electronic and acoustic occupancy. Peaks remain dry, buoyant, and club-functional rather than explosive.

# Scene & Origin Lore

Triad-Lurch Garage formed inside a former municipal taxi-dispatch and route-control office in Tbilisi that had been converted into shared rehearsal rooms. The building retained route boards, wall clocks, green desk lamps, numbered pigeonholes, old radio handsets, fare charts, glass service windows, metal filing drawers, and handwritten shift logs.

Panduri player **Nino Beridze** had been using one of the old dispatch rooms because its dry plaster walls preserved the difference between thumbed low-string attacks and nail-driven upper-string strokes. Her background included traditional ensemble repertoire, singer accompaniment, and contemporary chamber projects, but she was most interested in reducing strumming into independent attack roles.

London producer **Jay Mercer** arrived with a compact sampler, two-step drum library, sub-bass synth, and unfinished garage sketches. His first arrangement used sampled panduri chords on every offbeat. Nino objected because the instrument had become a timbral sticker. They returned to live playing and isolated one repeating chord figure. Jay muted his drums one by one until he realized the panduri itself could imply several missing percussion functions.

Percussionist **Dato Kapanadze** joined after developing frame-drum responses for Ghost Step moments. Keyboardist **Maya Sinclair** added sustained electric-piano fields that stayed behind the panduri rather than competing with it. Bass trombonist **Leon Grant** completed the ensemble with sparse low-register punctuation for section returns and Window Bars.

Their first performances were called **route tests**. Three small lamps behind Nino were labeled LOW, MID, and HIGH. A stage assistant illuminated the course carrying the strongest rhythmic role in each section. The lamps controlled nothing. They simply made the internal hand choreography visible to listeners who otherwise might hear the panduri as one blended strum.

The visual language developed around route-board green, taxi-amber, wet asphalt black, pale plaster, dark walnut, chrome hardware, burgundy upholstery, and fluorescent white. Wardrobe favors practical contemporary clothing—jackets, knit shirts, loose trousers, sneakers, simple dresses—rather than folk costume or clubwear caricature.

The unofficial rule is that if the entire groove can be preserved by sampling one panduri chord and repeating it, the arrangement has failed.

